<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maribor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marburg an der Drau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maribor is the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-styria">Lower Styria</a>. A medieval market town of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> with a largely German-speaking population, it was secured for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in 1918–1919 by Rudolf Maister's forces. It is a centre of wine-growing, claims the world's oldest living grapevine, and was a European Capital of Culture in 2012.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maribor.jpg" alt="Maribor" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vlada Republike Slovenije · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariborski_Glavni_trg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Maribor is the largest city of the traditional region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-styria">Lower Styria</a> and the economic, administrative, educational and cultural centre of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. The Drava River divides it, and two hills, Calvary Hill and Pyramid Hill, rise among vineyards. Settlement in the area goes back to the fifth millennium BCE, and in Roman times it lay in the province of Noricum on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A castle was built on Pyramid Hill to guard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a> against Hungarian raids, and it was first mentioned on 20 October 1164 as Castrum Marchburch. The settlement below it received town privileges in 1254 and grew rapidly after Rudolf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> defeated King Otakar II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1278. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are first mentioned in 1277 and had their own quarter and synagogue; Maximilian I ordered them out in 1496. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> besieged the town in 1480 and 1481 without success, and in 1532 a large Ottoman army under Suleiman attacked it and was repulsed. Fires and plague in the seventeenth century cost the town 35 per cent of its population.</p><p>The Southern Railway reached it in 1846, and in 1859 Bishop Anton Martin Slomšek moved the seat of the diocese to Maribor. In 1900 the city was 82.3 per cent German-speaking, though most of the surrounding land was Slovene. In November 1918 Rudolf Maister organised Slovene units that took control of the city from those who wished it to join German Austria. On 27 January 1919 Slovene troops fired on a German crowd, killing nine; who gave the order has never been established. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain</a> of 10 September 1919 recognised the city as part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>.</p><p>Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-styria">Lower Styria</a> in 1941 and expelled many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Maribor were deported. Allied bombing in the closing years of the war devastated some 47 per cent of the city area. After the war it became an industrial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and in 1991 it became part of independent Slovenia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Maribor Cathedral dates from the thirteenth century and the Maribor Synagogue from the fourteenth, which is described as the second oldest synagogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Lent Festival is held every June, and the house at Lent grows the grapevine Žametovka, over 400 years old, recorded in 2004 as the world's oldest. The University of Maribor was established in 1975. Maribor was a European Capital of Culture in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maribor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was then divided: Lower Styria with <b>Maribor</b> went to the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes and is now in Slovenia, while the rest became the Austrian state of Styria.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-styria">Duchy of Styria</a></li></ul></section>
